/-!
# The cohomology of the décalage at a point, and finite freeness of its stalks

Let `(X, O_X)` be a ringed space carrying a sheaf of ideals `ι : I ⟶ O_X` which is invertible
and a subsheaf, and let `F` be a cochain complex of `I`-torsion-free `O_X`-modules.  Tag 0F8N
identifies the cohomology sheaf `H^n(η_I F)` of the décalage with the twisted quotient
`I^{⊗ n} ⊗ (H^n(F)/H^n(F)[I])`.  This file is the *pointwise* form of that statement: at a
point `x` at which the cohomology of `F` has no `I`-torsion — that is, at which multiplication
by the ideal is injective on the twisted cohomology stalk — the comparison

`(I^{⊗ n} ⊗ H^n(F))_x ⟶ H^n(η_I F)_x`

of Tag 0F8N is already an isomorphism of `O_{X,x}`-modules, no quotient being needed.

The second half of the file is the notion of a stalk being finite free of a given rank over
`O_{X,x}` and its behaviour under that comparison: if `(I^{⊗ n} ⊗ H^n(F))_x` is finite free of
rank `r`, then so is `H^n(η_I F)_x`, and the rank is well defined as soon as `O_{X,x}` is
nonzero.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.IdealTorsion.Eta.etaCohomologyStalkEquiv`: the comparison of Tag 0F8N at a point, as
  an `O_{X,x}`-linear equivalence `(I^{⊗ n} ⊗ H^n(F))_x ≃ H^n(η_I F)_x`.
* `Stacks.IdealTorsion.Eta.IsFiniteFreeStalkOfRank`: the stalk of a sheaf of modules at a point
  is a finite free `O_{X,x}`-module of rank `r`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.IdealTorsion.Eta.shortExact_etaHomologyShortComplex`: the sequence
  `I^{⊗ (m+1)} ⊗ H^{m+1}(F)[I] ⟶ I^{⊗ (m+1)} ⊗ H^{m+1}(F) ⟶ H^{m+1}(η_I F)` is short exact.
  This repackages the two facts Tag 0F8N is assembled from — the comparison is an epimorphism
  and its kernel is the twisted torsion — as a single short exact sequence, which is the form
  that survives an exact functor.
* `Stacks.IdealTorsion.Eta.bijective_stalkMap_etaHomologyMap`: **the stalk form of Tag 0F8N.**
  At a point `x` where multiplication by the ideal is injective on `(I^{⊗ n} ⊗ H^n(F))_x`, the
  stalk of the comparison map of Tag 0F8N is bijective.
* `Stacks.IdealTorsion.Eta.IsFiniteFreeStalkOfRank.rank_eq`: over a nonzero stalk of the
  structure sheaf the rank of a finite free stalk is unique.
* `Stacks.IdealTorsion.Eta.isFiniteFreeStalkOfRank_idealPowerTerm`: **untwisting at a stalk.**
  If `I_x` is free of rank one then `(I^{⊗ n} ⊗ M)_x` is finite free of rank `r` exactly when
  `M_x` is, for every `n : ℤ`.
* `Stacks.IdealTorsion.Eta.isFiniteFreeStalkOfRank_one_of_isLocalRing`: on a ringed space whose
  stalks are local rings the stalk of an invertible sheaf of modules is free of rank one, which
  discharges the hypothesis of the previous item.
* `Stacks.IdealTorsion.Eta.isFiniteFreeStalkOfRank_one_of_isLocallyGeneratedByRegular`: the same
  conclusion on an *arbitrary* ringed space from the hypothesis of Situation 0GT4, that the
  ideal is locally generated by a section which is a nonzerodivisor.
* `Stacks.IdealTorsion.Eta.isFiniteFreeStalkOfRank_etaHomology`: if `(I^{⊗ n} ⊗ H^n(F))_x` is
  finite free of rank `r` then so is `H^n(η_I F)_x`.
* `Stacks.IdealTorsion.Eta.isFiniteFreeStalkOfRank_etaHomology_of_homology`: the same with the
  hypothesis on the untwisted cohomology — if `H^n(F)_x` is finite free of rank `r` then so is
  `H^n(η_I F)_x`.  This is the content of Tag 0GTA at the level of complexes.
* `Stacks.IdealTorsion.Eta.isFiniteFreeStalkOfRank_homology_leta`: for `M` an object of
  `D(O_X)` and the functor `derivedEta` of a choice of torsion-free resolutions, if `H^n(M)_x` is
  finite free of rank `r` and `I_x` is free of rank one then `H^n(Lη_I M)_x` is finite free of
  rank `r`.
* `Stacks.IdealTorsion.Eta.isFiniteFreeStalkOfRank_homology_derivedEtaOfRegular`: **Tag 0GTA in
  Situation 0GT4 itself**, for the functor `derivedEtaOfRegular` of Tag 0F8Q built from the
  situation, the torsion-free resolutions and the freeness of rank one of `I_x` being proved from
  it.
* `Stacks.IdealTorsion.Eta.rank_homology_derivedEtaOfRegular_eq`: **the clause "of the same
  rank" of Tag 0GTA**, in Situation 0GT4 itself — if `H^n(M)_x` is finite free of rank `r` and
  `H^n(Lη_I M)_x` of rank `s` then `s = r`.  This is where the hypothesis `O_{X,x} ≠ 0` of
  Tag 0GTA is used, and the only place it is needed.
* `Stacks.IdealTorsion.Eta.rank_homology_derivedEta_eq`: the same for `derivedEta`.
* `Stacks.IdealTorsion.Eta.rank_homology_leta_eq`: the ranks compared at a named resolution
  rather than at the functor.

## Implementation notes

**The torsion hypothesis.** The sheaf-level statement of Tag 0F8N carries no hypothesis on the
cohomology: the torsion is divided out, the quotient `H^n(F)/H^n(F)[I]` appearing on the left.
Its pointwise form must instead *assume* the torsion invisible at `x`, and the hypothesis used
here is that the stalk at `x` of the ideal-power inclusion

`idealPowerIncl : I^{⊗ (n+1)} ⊗ M ⟶ I^{⊗ n} ⊗ M`,

which is multiplication by the ideal, is injective for `M = H^n(F)`.  By
`Stacks.IdealTorsion.Eta.idealPowerAnnihilatorIsKernel` the kernel of that morphism is the
twisted torsion subsheaf, so the hypothesis says exactly that the twisted torsion has no germ
at `x`; in the language of clause (5) of Tag 0GT5 it is the assertion that a generator of the
stalk ideal `I_x` is a nonzerodivisor on the (twisted) cohomology stalk.  Assuming instead that
`H^n(F)` is `I`-torsion-free as a sheaf would make the pointwise statement vacuous, since then
the comparison of Tag 0F8N is an isomorphism of sheaves already.

**Why short exactness and not the isomorphism.** An exact functor carries a short exact
sequence to a short exact sequence, but it does not carry the quotient
`H^n(F) ↠ H^n(F)/H^n(F)[I]` to an isomorphism unless the torsion dies.  So what is transported
to the stalk is the short exact sequence `shortExact_etaHomologyShortComplex` rather than the
isomorphism `etaCohomologyIso` itself; the torsion hypothesis is then used exactly once, to see
that the first map of the transported sequence vanishes.

**Two spellings of the stalk.** Exactness of the stalk functor is available for
`AlgebraicGeometry.SheafOfModules.stalkFunctor`, whose values are modules over the
`RingCat`-valued stalk of the structure sheaf, while the stalk vocabulary of Tag 01CB used by
`EtaSheaf.lean` — `ringStalk`, `grpStalk`, `stalkMapL` — is stated over the `CommRingCat`-valued
`ringStalk X x`.  No comparison of the two filtered colimits is needed, exactly as in
`StacksFrontier/Algebra/Category/ModuleCat/Sheaf/KernelStalk.lean`: bijectivity is a property of
the underlying additive map, and that map is literally the same in both spellings, so the
conclusion of the exactness argument is transported by definitional equality and only then
packaged as a `ringStalk X x`-linear equivalence.

**Where the stalk of the ideal must be free.** Untwisting the factor `I^{⊗ n}` at a stalk needs
`I_x` to be *free* of rank one over `O_{X,x}`, not merely invertible: an invertible sheaf of
modules on a general ringed space has invertible stalks
(`Stacks.SheafOfModules.module_invertible_grpStalk`) but need not have free ones, which is
exactly the failure of the converse of Tag 0B8M recorded in
`StacksFrontier/Algebra/Category/ModuleCat/Sheaf/InvertibleRankOneLocal.lean`.  The untwisting
statements therefore take freeness of `I_x` as a hypothesis.  It is discharged in two
situations: when the stalks of `O_X` are local rings
(`isFiniteFreeStalkOfRank_one_of_isLocalRing`, so on every scheme), and in Situation 0GT4
itself, where the ideal is locally generated by a nonzerodivisor and hence trivialized on a
neighbourhood of every point.

**The functor `Lη_I` of Tag 0F8Q.** Tag 0GTA is a statement about the derived functor `Lη_I`
on `D(O_X)`, and the functor used is `Stacks.IdealTorsion.Eta.derivedEta`, the construction of
Tag 0F8Q; its input is the existence of termwise `I`-torsion-free resolutions of unbounded
complexes, which is the hypothesis `hres`.  The proof chooses such a resolution `F` of `M`, so
that `derivedEta_iso` identifies `Lη_I M` with the class of the chain-level décalage `η_I F`,
and the statement is then the complex-level one transported along
`DerivedCategory.homologyFunctorFactors`.  `derivedEta` is constructed at the standard model of
the derived category, so the two statements about `Lη_I` below are read at that model rather
than at the locally small one of
`StacksFrontier/Homology/DerivedCategory/LocallySmall.lean`.

**The two clauses of Tag 0GTA, and where the nonvanishing hypothesis goes.**  The source
concludes that `H^i(Lη_I M)_x` is finite free "of the same rank", which is two assertions: that
it is finite free at the rank of `H^i(M)_x`, and that no other rank is possible.  The first is
`isFiniteFreeStalkOfRank_homology_derivedEtaOfRegular` and needs nothing about `O_{X,x}`, the
rank being carried as a parameter `r` common to hypothesis and conclusion.  The second is
`rank_homology_derivedEtaOfRegular_eq` and is exactly where `O_{X,x} ≠ 0` is consumed, as
`Nontrivial (ringStalk X x)`: over the zero ring every module is free of every rank, so the
phrase "the rank" has no referent there.  Both are tagged, the two together being the source
statement under the source's own hypotheses and no others.

The rank statements are inside the `StandardDerivedCategory` section, at the same model of the
derived category as the finite-freeness ones, so that the conclusion of one is a hypothesis the
other accepts.  `rank_homology_leta_eq`, which predates them, is stated outside that section
and therefore at the locally small model, and additionally names a resolution `F` of `M` and
comparison isomorphisms `eM`, `eL` rather than speaking about the functor; it is kept because it
is the form available when a resolution is already in hand, but it is not the one that composes
with the tagged statements.

**Hypotheses, and the binders.** As in `EtaSheaf.lean` and `EtaSheafCohomology.lean` the
hypotheses on the ideal are `Mono ι` together with invertibility of `I`, that is condition (2)
of Tag 0GT3 rather than condition (1) demanded by Situation 0GT4; this is formally weaker, so
the statements are formally stronger.  The binders are repeated on every declaration rather
than collected in a `variable` command, because the notation `Oₓ` for the structure sheaf
mentions the space and so cannot be elaborated inside a `variable` command.

